WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:05.040
We don't want to get too dark here, but the
fact is, we all have a limited time in our

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.490
lives — so why not make the most of it with
a marathon of amazing ninja movies?

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:13.990
Pop some popcorn, sharpen your katana, and
come with us as we comb through the good,

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:18.930
the bad, and the completely inexplicable to
bring you the best ninja movies you must see

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.099
before you die.

00:00:21.099 --> 00:00:22.640
Heroes of the East

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.450
If you're looking for a place to jump on with
ninja movies, there aren't a lot of better

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:30.280
starting points than 1979's Heroes of the
East.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.010
Also known as Shaolin vs. Ninja, it follows
the time-tested formula of pitting a master

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.870
of one style against a group of rivals to
see whose kung fu reigns supreme.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:44.620
In this case, that master is the legendary
Gordon Liu — who was fresh off his breakthrough

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:50.310
performance as the hero of 36th Chamber of
Shaolin, and who would go on to international

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:54.350
fame as Pai Mei in Quentin Tarantino's Kill
Bill.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:55.989
Five Element Ninjas

00:00:55.989 --> 00:01:00.579
Any conversation about classic martial arts
movies usually leads to one of the most widely

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:05.239
accepted truisms of the genre: Five Deadly
Venoms rules hard.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:10.320
The 1982 follow-up Five Element Ninjas combines
everything good about that cult film, and

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.470
takes it about five steps further over the
top.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:18.610
While it's not as well-known as its predecessor,
Five Element Ninjas is every bit the blood-soaked

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:20.070
classic that Venoms is.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:24.950
The plot is pretty simple — a kung fu school
that was defeated in battle hires a color-coded,

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:30.880
element-themed ninja clan to get revenge on
the warriors that beat them — but honestly?

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.409
That hardly matters when you're getting glorious
fight scenes with ninjas appearing and disappearing

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:40.490
in a cloud of red smoke, or watching the heroes
celebrate tearing their enemies in half by

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.240
punching a giant boulder so hard that it explodes.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.010
Ninja III: The Domination

00:01:55.010 --> 00:02:00.600
If you want to see the '80s ninja explosion
in all of its bizarre, high-concept glory,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.990
you need to go straight to the third entry
in the infamous Cannon Films Ninja Trilogy.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:10.090
That's not to say Enter the Ninja and Revenge
of the Ninja aren't worth seeing, because

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.629
they definitely are.

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:14.200
"Wait, wait, ninja, wait.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.629
Why don't we speak like… civilized men."

00:02:17.629 --> 00:02:22.219
But the first two aren't necessary to enjoy
Ninja III: The Domination.

00:02:22.219 --> 00:02:27.329
After the opening that finds a ninja launching
a wildly violent assassination attempt on

00:02:27.329 --> 00:02:32.260
a golf course, the mysterious warrior ends
up dead — but not before his soul possesses

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:37.480
an aerobics instructor who suddenly finds
herself unwillingly moonlighting as a deadly

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.480
assassin.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.099
It's a twist on the usual ninja movie setup
that combines martial arts action with the

00:02:43.099 --> 00:02:47.950
setup of a horror movie, and the end result
is pure '80s madness.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.279
American Ninja 2: The Confrontation

00:02:50.279 --> 00:02:55.450
If you're only going to watch one of Cannon's
American Ninja films — and let's be real

00:02:55.450 --> 00:03:00.769
here, you should definitely only watch one
— the second installment is by far the best.

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:05.650
Michael Dudikoff returns as an Army Ranger
named Joe Armstrong who's investigating the

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.540
disappearances of a handful of marines from
a military base in a Caribbean paradise with

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.629
his sidekick, Curtis Jackson.

00:03:12.629 --> 00:03:17.889
It turns out those abducted jarheads are being
brainwashed and genetically altered into an

00:03:17.889 --> 00:03:22.870
army of super-ninjas, which means Armstrong
and Jackson have to ninja their way through

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:28.019
an entire base of brainwashed bad guys to
blow up the entire operation.

00:03:28.019 --> 00:03:33.059
It's goofy, full of bloodless knockouts and
over-the-top twists, but all that adds up

00:03:33.059 --> 00:03:35.670
to a movie that's uproariously entertaining.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:40.040
Just make sure you don't follow it up with
the incomprehensible American Ninja 3: Blood

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:41.040
Hunt.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.249
Pray For Death

00:03:42.249 --> 00:03:46.719
On one level, Pray For Death is basically
just Death Wish with Charles Bronson swapped

00:03:46.719 --> 00:03:48.310
out for Sho Kosugi.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:51.709
Look a little deeper, though, and there's
something in here that's a little bit smarter

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:53.979
than your average ninja revenge story.

00:03:53.979 --> 00:03:59.109
Make no mistake: there's a lot of ninja revenge
in this movie, with Kosugi playing the role

00:03:59.109 --> 00:04:04.189
of Akira, a highly trained ninja who leaves
his life of violence behind in order to travel

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:06.150
to America with his family.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.329
And open a restaurant.

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:11.849
Like pretty much all of Kosugi's ninja films,
the action is spectacular, but the really

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:16.419
compelling stuff here comes in how the movie
presents Akira as an immigrant in pursuit

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:20.780
of a peaceful new life in America, and the
contrast between the dream of success and

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:24.220
the barriers he encounters along the way.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:25.710
Ninja Assassin

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:30.720
On the off chance that you were worried Sho
Kosugi had stopped being a total badass after

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.360
the '80s, don't fret.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:37.740
In 2009, his starring role as the villain
of Ninja Assassin proved that he definitely

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:38.900
still had it.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:43.810
Produced by the Wachowskis and directed by
James McTeigue, Ninja Assassin cast Kosugi

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:49.850
as Lord Ozunu, the ruthless head of a ninja
clan that trains orphans from birth to become

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:51.400
killer ninjas.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:57.250
Unfortunately for Ozunu, his chosen successor,
Raizo, goes renegade, setting the stage for

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:02.130
exactly the kind of beautiful, visually stunning
fight scenes that you'd expect from people

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.380
who brought you The Matrix.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:05.840
The Octagon

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:10.310
If you only ever watch one Chuck Norris movie
in your life, it should be… well, it should

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.800
be Lone Wolf McQuade, if only because Chuck
Norris drives a pickup truck out of his own

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:14.480
grave.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.030
If you want one with ninjas, however, you
gotta go for The Octagon.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:31.760
Despite the lack of truck-based resurrection,
it's basically a perfect storm of a Chuck

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.760
Norris movie.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.740
Norris stars as Scott James, a bearded karate
master who finds himself caught up in a terrorist

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:37.740
plot.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:41.690
Since he just happens to be pals with a couple
of mercenaries who are also dealing with a

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:47.030
severe ninja problem, the whole thing climaxes
in a sequence where Scott James has to fight

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:51.870
his way through the ninjas' own lair, the
Octagon.

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.570
Azumi

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:58.930
Director Ryuhei Kitamura is probably best
known for his work on Godzilla: Final Wars,

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:03.361
but if you really want to see Kitamura take
on hyperviolent widespread destruction, you

00:06:03.361 --> 00:06:05.230
need to sit down and watch Azumi.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:10.580
Based on the manga of the same name, Azumi
offers incredibly stylish action, with practical

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:16.010
effects enhanced by CGI that only rarely ends
up feeling silly — and there's a twist at

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:19.900
the end that's truly delightful for fans of
ninja action.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:20.900
Izo

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:26.770
Takashi Miike's career as a director has been
all over the map, but the one thing that unites

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:31.200
all his films is a style that verges on the
surreal, creating movies that are often less

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.410
about the narrative and more about the experience.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:39.530
That's the case with Izo, which follows a
19th-century assassin through the afterlife,

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:43.980
punctuating violent encounters with big questions
about the nature of suffering.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:48.310
It's certainly not the fun martial arts romp
that you'll get with other films, but it's

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:52.780
definitely fascinating, and worth a watch
for the ninja obsessed.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.500
Miami Connection

00:06:54.500 --> 00:07:00.230
Made with a minuscule budget in 1987 by amateur
filmmaker, martial artist, and Dragon Sound

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:05.620
rhythm guitarist Y.K. Kim, Miami Connection
received incredibly poor reviews on its initial

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:10.720
release, but became an instant cult classic
after it was unearthed by the Alamo Drafthouse

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.030
in 2012.

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.520
It's easy to see why, too.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.450
The tortured drama, energetic amateur acting,
and spectacularly weird plot of a synthpop

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:23.620
band of martial artists throwing down against
a bunch of cocaine-dealing ninja bikers all

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:29.090
add up to an incredible amount of weird charm
that more than makes up for what this movie

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:30.820
lacks in technique.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:33.660
Watch it...or suffer the wrath of this guy:

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:42.930
Thanks for watching!

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.440
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:47.560
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

